#{[17592186199045 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199045 #edb/ref 1061 120128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199045 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71a8a715-2397-5052-a148-b7cc79853a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199045 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199046 #edb/ref 1061 120129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199046 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1887f6f2-227f-5082-89ee-2e84c56eb994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199046 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1064 "We can apply the logical operations of conjunction and disjunction to predicates.\n\n- The **conjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is a new predicate which we denote as follows: \n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\land Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)\n$$\n\nThis conjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true for a specific set of respective variable assignments. The conjunction is false if either $P$ or $Q$ is false.\n\n- The **disjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is written as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\lor Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)\n$$\n\nThis disjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true, $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true, or both are true, for a specific set of variable assignments. The disjunction is false if $P$ *and* $Q$ are false.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates:\n\n- $P(n):$ $n$ is even\n\n- $Q(n):$ $n$ is positive\n\nThen, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *and* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even and positive.\"\n\nOn the other hand, $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *or* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even or positive.\"\n\nWhen it's clear that $P(n)$ is a predicate in the variable $n,$ we sometimes omit the variables when writing them. So, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ and $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ can be written as $P \\land Q$ and $P \\lor Q,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1065 7984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c05b0e9a-b514-5c42-9d07-7ec2466b30be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nFrom the definition of the conjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\land Q$ is true if both $P$ and $Q$ are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\land Q$ is the *intersection* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\land Q} = T_P \\cap T_Q\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates defined over the set of positive integers:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $3$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $24$ is divisible by $x$\n\nWe start by finding the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_P = \\big\\{ 3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27 \\ldots \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P∧Q} & ={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27…\\}\\cap \\{1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,12,24\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1065 7985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74eb89b1-6654-56b5-9fef-718a3acfcb2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1067 "The Truth Set of a Conjunction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1064 "Conjunction and disjunction of predicates are generalizations of logical operations on statements. Moreover, the properties of these operations on statements can be translated to corresponding operations on predicates. \n\nIn particular, the idempotent, absorption, associative, and commutative laws also apply to conjunction and disjunction of predicates. \n\nSo, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n),$ $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ and $R(z_1,z_2\\ldots,z_p),$ we have the following:\n\n- *The Idempotent Laws*: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧P≡P∨P≡P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Associative Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∧R) & ≡(P∧Q)∧R \\\\ P∨(Q∨R) & ≡(P∨Q)∨R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Commutative Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧Q & ≡Q∧P \\\\ P∨Q & ≡Q∨P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Distributive Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R) \\\\ P∨(Q∧R) & ≡(P∨Q)∧(P∨R)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *The Absorption Laws*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∧(P∨Q) & ≡P \\\\ P∨(P∧Q) & ≡P\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1065 7989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ad96982-4e36-52f9-816a-db64ba277433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1067 "Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction of Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1064 "Once again, let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. \n\nFrom the definition of the disjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\lor Q$ is true if $P$ is true, or $Q$ is true, or both are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\lor Q$ must be the *union* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\lor Q} = T_P \\cup T_Q\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the following shaded regions that represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ \n\n![](images/ce/ce71e62503acd1c82f6bba7e1d9c83c40f0a98f04c689c3ccd58e7a99a814856.png)\n\nTo get the truth set of $P(x,y) \\lor Q(x,y),$ we take all the points that belong to $T_P$ or $T_Q,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9c/9c528c5fe390bd1f22cb473c01b768e83f5b3a35d97eb7e670c6734b8cb2c2f8.png)\n\n**Note:** The points where the *dashed* line meets the *solid* curve belong to $T_P \\cup T_Q$ since these points lie in $T_P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1065 8140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d1b797a-ff89-514d-a0df-87d6858b45e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1067 "The Truth Set of a Disjunction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236063 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236063 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236063 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "268106be-19f1-5f82-813d-950192dc5718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236841 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(-\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{9}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236841 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236841 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a348d55a-36a9-5af2-81e6-6f7585bd95e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237129 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237129 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237129 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cf31be4e-0647-5297-be76-a4721eb22f2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237197 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{9}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237197 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237197 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9ca6f54-6773-56f6-8dd0-f043a63dc54a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237416 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(-\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, radius: $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237416 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237416 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc2a1ef9-6f90-5fd1-aa22-221a29a4b907" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186236063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237503 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2e70a388-068c-5ad0-9ccf-1ecef596f218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247546 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d83e583-d218-50f4-abb7-474c6d01c304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247961 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{1}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247961 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247961 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d047995-440b-51a6-837d-6a9c0a9dea33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248013 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248013 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248013 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4217347b-9b32-5330-8c51-bcd370f084c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248680 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248680 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248680 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c7776ae-27e1-5fb2-91d8-1d3d11b460fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248983 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{1}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248983 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248983 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aea67226-a698-5829-a7e0-c9089694e776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186247961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186248983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186248013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249818 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ff430e6-479c-5fff-ae3c-19a79a5cfe3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250242 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37affd24-3b4f-536c-8927-723157249685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250242 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{9}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+\\frac{1}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270583 #edb/ref 1080 "${v}_{1}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270583 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270583 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "95ad0c30-25aa-5214-9fc7-c939f04b78e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270599 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270599 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270599 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "975fd28e-adf6-5603-b413-9e3981f4ba92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270755 #edb/ref 1080 "${v}_{3}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270755 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270755 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a602827a-7757-58ef-8992-cd274f1c3f2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271380 #edb/ref 1080 "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f01d63bf-360f-5027-9b52-9a5dc8da2b84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271402 #edb/ref 1080 "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271402 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271402 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f2beaf42-65fa-5bab-8cbb-58886c446972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271821 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "abea13b0-61b2-513e-8903-85f6a044cb51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272101 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272101 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272101 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272101 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186272101 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "886a7d65-d33d-599b-a7ad-97a12c1790fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272101 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-11)=-64\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+(-14)\\cdot 0+(-6)\\cdot 5=-60\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+14\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+14\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-11)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272824 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272824 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272824 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31063f08-1e78-5379-8242-cacf1a1d6a7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273321 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273321 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273321 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c4099a2-b1f7-512a-bb2c-d7f0465c2381" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273722 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(12,-3)$, radius: $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9a8b464f-a745-52e7-91ac-bd4ce713ef33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273735 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(12,3)$, radius: $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273735 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273735 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9bac1bca-1a7c-516f-8ec8-2ff395b49a60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273919 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(3,12)$, radius: $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b13ca8a9-fd5e-51c8-a5cb-c5ca2dfa49f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273975 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273975 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273975 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b713ce94-48fa-5ac2-95e8-75f2acb1782d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274203 #edb/ref 1080 "center: $(-12,-3)$, radius: $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186274203 #edb/ref 1082 #e